A linked list is a linear data structure where each element is a separate object with a data part and a link part. The link part points to the next element in the list. To create a linked list in C++, we start by defining a node structure, which should have a data member and a next pointer:

```cpp
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};
```

Next, we can create a class for a linked list that includes functions to manipulate the list:

```cpp
class LinkedList {
private:
    Node* head;
public:
    LinkedList() {
        head = nullptr;
    }
    void insertAtHead(int value) {
        Node* newNode = new Node();
        newNode->data = value;
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = newNode;
            head->next = nullptr;
        }
        else {
            newNode->next = head;
            head = newNode;
        }
    }
    void display() {
        Node* temp = head;
        while (temp != nullptr) {
            cout << temp->data << " ";
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
};
```

Here, we have created a `LinkedList` class that has a private member `head`, that points to the first element of the list. The class has a constructor that initializes `head` to `nullptr`. It also has two public functions: `insertAtHead` to insert a new element at the beginning of the list, and `display` to display the entire list.

Here is an example of how to create and manipulate a linked list:

```cpp
int main()
{
    LinkedList list;
    list.insertAtHead(1);
    list.insertAtHead(2);
    list.insertAtHead(3);
    list.display();
    return 0;
}
```

This will create a linked list with three elements and display it. The output will be: `3 2 1`.

